e>Diagnosis and treatment of enterovesical fistulae</article-title><source><italic>American Surgeon</italic></source><year>1992</year><volume>58</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>258</fpage><lpage>263</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="other">2-s2.0-0026539464</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1586086</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B10"><label>10</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Schwaibold</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Popiel</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Geist</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Hartung</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Oral intake of poppy seed: a reliable and simple method for diagnosing vesico-enteric fistula</article-title><source><italic>Journal of Urology</italic></source><year>2001</year><volume>166</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>530</fpage><lpage>531</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="other">2-s2.0-0034934498</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11458060</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B11"><label>11</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>West</surname><given-names>CF</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Vesico enteric fistulas</article-title><source><italic>Surgical Clinics of North America</italic></source><year>1973</year><volume>53</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>565</fpage><lpage>570</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="other">2-s2.0-0015901408</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">4707074</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B12"><label>12</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wedell</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Banzhaf</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Chaoui</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Fischer</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Reichmann</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Surgical management of complicated colonic diverticulitis</article-title><source><italic>British Journal of Surgery</italic></source><year>1997</year><volume>84</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>380</fpage><lpage>383</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="other">2-s2.0-0030933921</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9117315</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="fig1" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>The urethrocutaneous fistula (horizontal arrow A) and the malalignment of the fractured left femur (vertical arrow B; facing downwards) are shown.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.UROLOGY2013-763032.001"/></fig><fig id="fig2" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Micturating cystourethrogram radiograph showing contrast intravasation into the sigmoid colon during the filling phase of the cystography. Black arrow pointing at the vesicocolonic junction.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.UROLOGY2013-763032.002"/></fig><fig id="fig3" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p> (a) Intraoperative photograph showing the sigmoid/vesical junction (arrow) before it was brought down. (b) Intraoperative photograph showing the bladder (arrow) with a Foley catheter going into the sigmoid colon (catheter balloon shown).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.UROLOGY2013-763032.003"/></fig></floats-group></article>